{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"LEAF 8ce5 — phase 4e VALIDATION AGAINST HELD-OUT ORACLE.\n\nImplementer returned green on the happy path in 2 attempts (473-line script, 917-line manifest,\nMakefile wired after gen_env_registry --check, docs section added). Its own reported failure was\ninstructive and it fixed it without touching tests: tests/unit/conftest.py:100 autouse fixture\nrebinds rebar.append_session_log to a (*_args, **_kwargs) stub tier-wide, so reading signatures off\nthe facade attribute reported the STUB's params. Fixed by resolving the SHIPPED definition from the\nsubmodule whose __module__ matches. That is a real flakiness source the behavioral oracle would not\notherwise have surfaced.\n\nRESTORED FULL ORACLE: 19 passed / 4 FAILED. The hold-out worked — both failures are genuine missing\nbehavior, not bad tests:\n 1. test_declared_divergence_with_a_blank_reason_fails (x3 params): a divergence block whose reason\n    is missing/empty/whitespace passes unnoticed on any non-'exact' tool, because the reason is only\n    consulted on the parameter-parity path (which the implementer scoped to 'exact' correspondences —\n    a defensible and well-documented decision I am KEEPING). The hole: the mandatory-reason\n    convention has no schema-level enforcement, so a blank declaration on a co_names/mcp_only entry\n    is silently accepted. Same rule check_config_reads.py applies to '# read-via:' (a bare marker is\n    itself an error).\n 2. test_mcp_only_tools_must_be_declared_as_such: a descriptor claiming correspondence='exact' while\n    carrying library_symbol=null is incoherent by the script's own definitions, and is accepted.\n    Matters because the manifest is HAND-EDITABLE (humans write the divergence reasons into it), so a\n    bad hand-edit must fail loudly rather than silently disabling a check.\n\nACTION: re-hid the held-out file, then re-dispatched the SAME implementer with a DESCRIPTION of the\ntwo missing contracts (never the withheld tests verbatim, per protocol) plus a seam hint\n(_validate_manifest helper) so the additions do not breach the LOC target or complexity ceiling.\nPer 4e-bis this is normal held-out iteration, NOT an escalation: a first held-out edge failure is the\noracle working as intended.\n\nIMPLEMENTER FINDING WORTH KEEPING: the ticket's claim of ONE real divergence (edit_ticket) is WRONG —\nthere are SIX after normalizing repo_root away: edit_ticket, create_ticket (return_alias, source,\n_creation_channel), create_identity (return_alias, tags, _creation_channel), fsck (report_only),\nsearch (full), show_ticket (include_inbound). Each is real (the tool pins the value at the call site\nrather than exposing it). It declared all six with source-cited reasons rather than inventing a\nbroader normalization rule to force the count to one — the right call, and it goes in the residual\nnotes as a ticket-accuracy correction.\nSecond correction: regeneration could NOT be documented in docs/generated-artifacts.md's existing\ntables because tests/unit/test_generated_surface_markers.py pins both tables row-for-row to hardcoded\nregistries; a third section was added instead so neither pinned table breaks."},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1788316193481642001,"uuid":"cae1795d-ceaa-4831-8a33-34c65d4ded01"}